.TH BLOCKSCAN 1 "July 2026" "1.0.0" "Blockscan Manual"
.SH NAME
blockscan \- detect bot-blocking and map crawlable domains
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blockscan
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fI<URL>\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBblockscan\fR is a fast, concurrent CLI tool written in Rust to rapidly determine if a target website blocks automated bot crawling (e.g., via Cloudflare, 403 Forbidden, or Rate Limiting).
If the website permits crawling, it seamlessly discovers and recursively lists other crawlable links within the same domain.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-d, \-\-depth\fR \fI<DEPTH>\fR
Maximum crawl depth. Specify 0 for unlimited. (Default: 2)
.TP
\fB\-c, \-\-concurrency\fR \fI<CONCURRENCY>\fR
Maximum concurrent requests limit. (Default: 10)
.TP
\fB\-u, \-\-user-agent\fR \fI<USER_AGENT>\fR
Custom User-Agent string to use for requests. (Default: blockscan/1.0.0 (Bot))
.TP
\fB\-j, \-\-json\fR
Output results in JSON format.
.TP
\fB\-\-plain\fR
Output results in raw plain text format, without UI elements or tables.
.TP
\fB\-\-no-color\fR
Disable colored terminal output.
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help information.
.TP
\fB\-V, \-\-version\fR
Print version information.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Basic scan of a website:
.B blockscan google.com
.TP
Scan with increased concurrency and depth:
.B blockscan --depth 3 --concurrency 50 google.com
.TP
Output results as JSON and pipe to jq:
.B blockscan google.com --json | jq '.'
.SH AUTHOR
Built with Rust.
